<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<!-- Exactly three entitlements, and each arrives with the feature that needs it.

     `com.apple.developer.applesignin` is what lets the app present an
     `ASAuthorizationAppleIDRequest`. It is claimed now because this slice ships
     the real system Sign in with Apple button and the hardened server exchange
     behind it; it was absent through the previous three slices for the same
     reason it is present now — an entitlement is a claim to the OS and to the
     user, and each one lands with the functional slice that earns it.

     `Default` (rather than `Sign In With Apple`) is the plain capability: this
     app is not the primary of an app group that shares an Apple ID relationship
     with another app.

     `com.apple.developer.associated-domains = [applinks:relayium.com]` is the
     second, and it lands by the same rule: the app now ROUTES the two link
     shapes relayium.com actually serves. `onOpenURL` is wired at the scene root,
     `AppDeepLinkCoordinator` selects the tab and prefills the field, and
     `AppDeepLinkCoordinatorTests` drives that against real models. Before this
     slice the entitlement would have been a claim with nothing behind it.

     One domain, one service, and both narrower than they look. `applinks:` and
     not `*.relayium.com`, because a wildcard would extend the trust boundary to
     every subdomain the service may later point somewhere else. No
     `activitycontinuation:`, which is Handoff between devices and something this
     app does not do. And no `webcredentials:` — password AutoFill is an
     association this app's sign-in form does not use. The SITE's association
     file matches on that point: it names this app under `applinks` only, and
     keeps its `webcredentials` list to the Mac app it was already permitting.
     AutoFill is a separate permission and would arrive with the feature that
     wants it, on both sides.

     A verified Universal Link is the only way in. There is deliberately no
     `CFBundleURLTypes` in `Info.plist`: a custom scheme is unauthenticated —
     any app can register `relayium://` — and a link handoff is a trust boundary.
     `parseAppDeepLink` then refuses again on its own terms (https, exactly this
     host, no userinfo, no non-443 port, one of two paths), so a mis-provisioned
     association cannot widen what the app will act on.

     Associated Domains needs two more things a SIGNED build supplies: the
     com.relayium.mac App ID carrying that capability, and relayium.com serving
     an `apple-app-site-association` that names `7PVYUG4YQS.com.relayium.mac`
     under `applinks` — which `web/public/.well-known/apple-app-site-association`
     already does, because that identifier is the Mac app's and this app now
     shares it. Neither is proved by a simulator build: the association is
     fetched and verified by the OS at install time, so link routing is on the
     list of things that still need a real device.

     The identifier changed under this comment and the site file did not, which
     is worth being exact about. iOS moved onto the macOS bundle id so both
     platforms sit in ONE universal-purchase App Store record, which Apple
     requires to share a single Bundle ID. `7PVYUG4YQS.com.relayium.mac` was
     already in the `applinks` list for the Mac app, so this app's routing is
     covered by an entry that predates the migration. The list ALSO still names
     `7PVYUG4YQS.com.relayium.app`, and that entry is deliberately left alone:
     development builds carrying the old identifier are installed on real
     devices, and removing it would break their link routing for no gain. It is
     backward compatibility, not a second shipping app.

     A SIGNED build additionally needs the com.relayium.mac App ID to carry the
     Sign in with Apple capability in the iOS provisioning profile it is signed
     with. The App ID itself already carries the capability — it was enabled for
     macOS — but a capability on an App ID and a capability inside the profile a
     binary embeds are two different facts, and only the second travels with an
     install. The SERVER half is already in place and needs no change: a fresh
     read-only production check on 2026-09-03 found RELAYIUM_APPLE_CLIENT_IDS
     admitting com.relayium.mac alongside com.relayium.app and the web Services
     ID, and the native route refuses the Services ID audience on its own terms.
     None of that is something this file can establish, and none of it is proved
     by a simulator build.

     The bearer token and the stored-link keys still live in this app's OWN
     default per-app keychain access group — the one every app gets — which is
     why no `keychain-access-groups` entitlement appears here. Naming a group
     would claim a credential share with another app that does not exist, and on
     a signed device build it would fail with `errSecMissingEntitlement`
     (-34018).

     `PhotosPicker` likewise needs neither an entitlement nor an
     `NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription`: it runs out of process and hands back only
     the items the user picked, so this app never has library access to ask for.

     `com.apple.security.application-groups = [group.com.relayium.app]` is the
     third, and it is the Share Extension's. `RelayiumShare.appex` copies what
     the user shared into that container and this app reads it back; a group is
     the only way two processes in one app can share a directory at all.

     The group kept its name when the bundle identifiers changed, and that is
     deliberate rather than an oversight. An App Group identifier is a
     registered container, not a derivative of the bundle id: it is provisioned
     on its own, it is what the portal already authorizes for BOTH this app and
     `com.relayium.mac.ShareIOS`, and renaming it would strand every draft an
     installed build has already staged. `group.com.relayium.app` is therefore a
     live identity that outlived the record migration, and `AppGroup.swift` is
     unchanged by it. The same is true one layer up, with one distinction worth
     stating precisely: `AppEnvironment`'s iOS keychain SERVICE label is still
     `com.relayium.app`. A service label is a lookup key, not a signed
     capability, and keeping it is continuity with the `com.relayium.mac`
     iOS/TestFlight lineage — the installs that already wrote their items under
     the shared bundle id — and with the existing code and tests that name it.
     What it is NOT is a migration from the retired identity. Moving the main
     bundle from `com.relayium.app` to `com.relayium.mac` moves this app's
     IMPLICIT DEFAULT keychain access group with it, from
     `7PVYUG4YQS.com.relayium.app` to `7PVYUG4YQS.com.relayium.mac`, so items a
     separately installed `com.relayium.app` development app wrote are neither
     carried over nor exposed here: a matching service label does not reach
     across access groups. That is the correct outcome, and reaching them would
     require an explicit `keychain-access-groups` entitlement that this file
     deliberately does not add.

     The App Group arrives ALONE, and the pairing an earlier version of this comment
     predicted — an App Group plus a shared keychain group — turned out to be
     wrong by one. The extension holds no credential and mints no key: it stages
     plaintext copies and stops. The content key for a shared draft is generated
     HERE, when the user presses Send, and filed under the pending job in this
     app's own default keychain group. So `keychain-access-groups` is still
     absent, and adding it would be a credential share with a process that has
     nothing to put in it.

     What the group container holds is worth being precise about, because it is
     readable by both processes for as long as a draft is waiting: plaintext
     copies of files the user deliberately shared, plus a plan naming them, their
     sizes and an opaque index. No account id, no bearer, no key, and no path
     from the device the copies were made from.

     Still absent, each with the slice that would earn it: background modes
     (transfers surviving suspension), push, IAP.

     Local network is a different case from all of those, and the reason this
     file is nonetheless unchanged by it is worth stating precisely: everything
     Local Nearby declares is an Info.plist matter, not a signed capability.

     DISCOVERY is Bonjour. iOS Nearby is RelayiumLocalPeerKit's
     NetworkLocalPeerTransport, behind LocalNearbyEnvironment, publishing and
     browsing exactly one product-specific service type on the local link and
     joining no room on any server — so the roster is this network rather than a
     public address some hub observed, and no address is ever scanned or
     enumerated. Declaring that service is what NSBonjourServices does, and it
     lives in Info.plist beside NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription. The multicast
     entitlement is a different thing again and stays absent:
     com.apple.developer.networking.multicast is for an app sending its own
     multicast or broadcast traffic, which this app does not send — Bonjour's
     mDNS is the system resolver's, not this process's.

     TRANSFER is local too, and that is the half an earlier version of this
     comment got wrong. Once the user picks a device, every realtime lane builds
     its peer connection with iceTransportPolicy = .all, so the pair iOS
     actually settles on is routinely a unicast socket to the peer's address on
     this subnet — and iOS 14 and later gate that behind Local Network access.
     R3-F shipped the Nearby tab with no NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription, which
     made the local path fail rather than prompt: retained physical runs
     0af36138 and 56e78dbf recorded iOS/iPadOS 26 withholding the prompt
     entirely while iPadOS 18 masked the omission.

     Both declarations live in Info.plist rather than here because Local Network
     is a user-consented protected resource, not a signed capability: an app
     states WHY it wants one and WHICH service it browses, and the system asks
     the user. So the three entitlements below are still exactly the three this
     app has earned, and three suites hold that line from the other side.
     IOSLocalNetworkPermissionTests: purpose string present, matching its two
     localizations, and absent from the share extension. IOSSurfaceGuardTests:
     NSBonjourServices is exactly this app's one service type, and multicast,
     wifi-info, background networking and every address-scanning API stay out.
     LocalNearbyModuleBoundaryTests: the browse itself stays inside
     RelayiumLocalPeerKit rather than becoming a second discovery surface. -->
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.developer.applesignin</key>
	<array>
		<string>Default</string>
	</array>
	<key>com.apple.developer.associated-domains</key>
	<array>
		<string>applinks:relayium.com</string>
	</array>
	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>group.com.relayium.app</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
